# Line endings are normalised to LF, everywhere, whatever wrote the file.
#
# On 2026-10-28 fourteen scripts under support/ and firmware/ turned up rewritten
# CRLF and stripped of their executable bit, with no content change at all -- a
# Windows-side tool touching the WSL checkout. Committed, each would have reached
# the datalogger with a `#!/bin/bash\r` shebang and died with "bad interpreter".
# Nothing prevented it: core.autocrlf was unset, which leaves the line endings to
# whatever happened to write the file last.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text, and `eol=lf` fixes the checkout to LF
# on every platform. The damage then normalises away on the next `git add` instead
# of landing in a commit.
#
# Note what this does NOT cover: the executable bit. Git tracks the mode itself and
# no attribute restores it, so a `mode change 100755 => 100644` in `git status` is
# still worth a second look before committing -- `chmod 755` is the fix.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: never converted, never diffed as text. A font blob or a packed
# framebuffer has no line endings -- only bytes that happen to be 0x0D, and
# "normalising" one corrupts it silently.
*.png      -text
*.raw      -text
*.bin      -text
*.ttf      -text
*.u8g2font -text

# Vendored verbatim, CRLF included: this is upstream's SIL licence text, and the
# only tracked file that already carries CRLF. Normalising it would show as us
# rewriting someone else's licence.
draw-display/fonts/OFL.txt -text
